Annotations: |
  This cell defines Nest's built-in HTTP exception hierarchy: a base
  `IntrinsicException` (a plain error the default exception filter does not log),
  `HttpException` (carries an HTTP status code and a JSON response body), and one
  concrete subclass per common HTTP status code. core/router's exception-handling
  path and both platform HTTP adapters depend on these types to recognise and format
  framework-level errors.

---

"IntrinsicException()":
  location: intrinsic.exception.ts
  annotations: |
    Marker base class for errors considered part of normal application control flow
    rather than unexpected failures. The default exception filter skips logging an
    error whose prototype chain includes this type.

"IntrinsicException::HttpException(response: string | Object<string, any>, status: number, options: Object<string, any>)":
  location: http.exception.ts
  annotations: |
    Base type for every HTTP-aware exception the framework throws or lets user code
    throw. Carries the JSON response body and the HTTP status code the router's
    exception handler uses to answer the request.

    `response`: string message, or an object used verbatim as the JSON response body
    `status`: HTTP status code returned to the client
    `options`: carries a chained original error (cause) and an opaque error code

    Algorithm:
    1. On construction, derive the exception message from `response` (its own
       message property, the string itself, or a title-cased form of the
       constructor name as a last resort)
    2. Set the exception name to the concrete constructor's name so logs and stack
       traces identify the specific exception subclass, not the generic base type
    3. Copy the cause and error code from `options` onto the instance when present
  methods:
    "getResponse() -> body: string | Object<string, any>": |
      Return the JSON response body (or plain message) this exception was
      constructed with.
    "getStatus() -> status: number": |
      Return the HTTP status code this exception was constructed with.

"HttpException::BadRequestException(objectOrError: any, descriptionOrOptions: string | Object<string, any>)":
  location: bad-request.exception.ts
  annotations: |
    400 Bad Request. `objectOrError` becomes the response body's message;
    `descriptionOrOptions` is either a short error-description string or an options
    object (cause, error code). Every other concrete exception in this directory
    (`UnauthorizedException`, `ForbiddenException`, `NotFoundException`,
    `InternalServerErrorException`, and the remaining status-code exceptions) follows
    this identical constructor shape and status-code binding pattern.

"HttpException::UnauthorizedException(objectOrError: any, descriptionOrOptions: string | Object<string, any>)":
  location: unauthorized.exception.ts
  annotations: |
    401 Unauthorized, constructed identically to `BadRequestException`.

"HttpException::ForbiddenException(objectOrError: any, descriptionOrOptions: string | Object<string, any>)":
  location: forbidden.exception.ts
  annotations: |
    403 Forbidden, constructed identically to `BadRequestException`. Thrown by the
    framework's guard pipeline when a guard denies a request.

"HttpException::NotFoundException(objectOrError: any, descriptionOrOptions: string | Object<string, any>)":
  location: not-found.exception.ts
  annotations: |
    404 Not Found, constructed identically to `BadRequestException`. Thrown by the
    router when no route matches the incoming request.

"HttpException::InternalServerErrorException(objectOrError: any, descriptionOrOptions: string | Object<string, any>)":
  location: internal-server-error.exception.ts
  annotations: |
    500 Internal Server Error, constructed identically to `BadRequestException`.

---

